md deletes the comment, and the two lists then merge into one.

This is defect 4 with a structural consequence, and it is worse than the
other defect 4 cases. Those lose the HTML block alone. Here the loss also
changes a block the user did not touch: md prints

    - alpha
    - bravo

    - charlie
    - delta

and `md blocks` reads that back as a single "unordered list(4 items) L1-5".
Two blocks became one, thus every block index below this point moves, and a
later `md remove 2` deletes the wrong thing.

The cause is defect 4: MarkdownBlock has no case for raw HTML, so parseNode
returns nil for an html_block and format never writes it back. Fixing
defect 4 fixes this case.
